---
import Studio from "../../layouts/Studio.astro";
import RelatedPosts from "../../components/RelatedPosts.astro";
import { SITE, DEFAULT_OG, CALENDLY_URL, fmtDate, iso, POSTS } from "../../lib/blog.mjs";
import { coverBase, coverSrcset, COVER_SIZES_POST } from "../../lib/images.mjs";

const slug = "scarcity-by-design-hand-numbered-drops";
const idx = POSTS.findIndex((p) => p.slug === slug);
const post = POSTS[idx];

const canonical = `${SITE}/blog/${slug}/`;
const ogImage = post.coverImage ? `${SITE}${post.coverImage}` : DEFAULT_OG;
const primaryTag = post.tags[0] || "Notes";
const readMins = post.readMins;
const showUpdated = post.updatedDate && +new Date(post.updatedDate) > +new Date(post.pubDate);
const updatedLabel = showUpdated ? `Updated ${fmtDate(post.updatedDate)}` : null;

const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BlogPosting",
      "@id": `${canonical}#post`,
      headline: post.title,
      description: post.description,
      datePublished: iso(post.pubDate),
      dateModified: iso(post.updatedDate || post.pubDate),
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Divyansh Sood", url: `${SITE}/`, "@id": `${SITE}/#person` },
      publisher: {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "@id": `${SITE}/#studio`,
        name: "Divyansh Sood® Studio",
        logo: { "@type": "ImageObject", url: `${SITE}/icon.svg` },
      },
      isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", "@id": `${SITE}/#website` },
      mainEntityOfPage: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
      image: ogImage,
      articleSection: primaryTag,
      keywords: post.tags.join(", "),
      wordCount: 690,
      timeRequired: `PT${readMins}M`,
      speakable: {
        "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
        cssSelector: [".post-title", ".post-lead"],
      },
      inLanguage: "en",
      url: canonical,
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Blog", item: `${SITE}/blog/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: post.title, item: canonical },
      ],
    },
  ],
});
---

<Studio
  title={post.seoTitle ?? `${post.title} · Divyansh Sood`}
  description={post.description}
  canonical={canonical}
  keywords={post.tags.join(", ")}
  ogType="article"
  ogTitle={post.title}
  ogImage={ogImage}
  ogImageW={1200}
  ogImageH={750}
  articlePublished={iso(post.pubDate)}
  articleModified={iso(post.updatedDate || post.pubDate)}
  tags={post.tags}
  preloadImage={{ href: coverBase(post.coverImage), srcset: coverSrcset(post.coverImage), sizes: COVER_SIZES_POST }}
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <main>
    <header class="post-hero">
      <div class="post-hero-bg" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <div class="eyebrow">
          <span>{primaryTag}</span>
          <span>{fmtDate(post.pubDate)}</span>
          {updatedLabel && <span class="post-updated">{updatedLabel}</span>}
          <span>{readMins} min read</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="post-title">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">{post.description}</p>
        <div class="post-meta">
          <span>By Divyansh Sood</span><span>{fmtDate(post.pubDate)}</span><span>{readMins} min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    {post.coverImage && (
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <figure class="post-cover">
          <img
            src={coverBase(post.coverImage)}
            srcset={coverSrcset(post.coverImage)}
            sizes={COVER_SIZES_POST}
            alt={post.coverAlt ?? post.title}
            width="1200"
            height="750"
            fetchpriority="high"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
    )}

    <article class="wrap narrow post-body">
      <p>There are two ways to sell apparel online.</p>
<p>One: stock deep, restock forever, compete on availability and price. The store’s job is to never be out of your size.</p>
<p>Two: make a fixed number, number each one, never make it again. The store’s job is to make you feel you’ll miss it.</p>
<p>Those are not the same business with different marketing. They’re different businesses — and they need different software. I learned exactly how different building <a href="https://www.divyanshsood.com/projects/cultxberserk/">CultXberserk</a>, a Berserk-inspired label where every garment is hand-numbered 0001 to 0666 and never reprinted.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="scarcity-only-works-if-its-real">Scarcity only works if it’s real</h2>
<p>The fastest way to kill a drop brand is fake scarcity. “Only 3 left!” countdown timers that reset on refresh. “Limited edition” that quietly restocks next month. Customers smell it instantly, and the moment they do, the premium evaporates.</p>
<p>Real scarcity is a constraint you actually honour:</p>
<ul>
<li>A fixed run, stated up front — 666 units, not “limited”</li>
<li>A visible number on the product itself — 0042 of 0666</li>
<li>A public commitment to never reprint, and then keeping it</li>
</ul>
<p>That last one is a business decision, not a design one. You are deliberately leaving money on the table on the best-sellers. That’s the cost of the model — and it’s exactly what makes the model work.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="the-story-has-to-live-next-to-the-buy-button">The story has to live next to the buy button</h2>
<p>For a commodity store, the product page is specs and a price. For a drop brand, the product page is where the <em>meaning</em> gets made.</p>
<p>CultXberserk’s tees carry a “Brand of Sacrifice” seal sewn into the neckline at the C7 vertebra — a reference to the manga’s lore, not decoration. That detail only sells if the page <em>tells</em> you it’s there and why it matters. The lore, the numbering, the origin — all of it has to sit inches from the add-to-cart, not buried in a separate “about” page nobody visits.</p>
<p>This is the part themes get wrong. They treat the product page as a transaction. A drop brand needs it to be a small piece of theatre.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="what-the-software-actually-has-to-do">What the software actually has to do</h2>
<p>Hand-numbered drops break assumptions baked into off-the-shelf stores:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unit-level identity.</strong> Not “200 in stock” but 200 <em>distinct, numbered</em> items. Inventory has to track individuals, not just a count.</li>
<li><strong>Drop state, not just stock state.</strong> Upcoming, live, sold out, archived — and the page should look different in each, building anticipation before and prestige after.</li>
<li><strong>No restock logic.</strong> Sold out means sold out, permanently. The “notify me when back” flow that every store assumes? You have to deliberately remove it.</li>
<li><strong>Provenance.</strong> Buyers of numbered goods want proof — which number they got, when, that it’s authentic.</li>
</ul>
<p>None of that is a standard app. It’s why drop brands so often end up custom: the model itself is custom.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="what-this-teaches-every-d2c-brand">What this teaches every D2C brand</h2>
<p>You don’t have to sell 666 units to use what makes drops work:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Make a real promise and keep it.</strong> Manufactured urgency is a tax on trust. A constraint you actually honour is a moat.</li>
<li><strong>Sell the meaning, not just the object.</strong> The most defensible premium is the story only you can tell.</li>
<li><strong>Let the model shape the software.</strong> If your brand idea is genuinely different, an off-the-shelf store will quietly sand it back into something ordinary — the same reason <a href="/blog/storefront-that-feels-like-the-brand/">a themed storefront rarely feels like the brand</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>That third point is the one that decides the build. A drop mechanic, a numbered edition, a waitlist that actually holds — those are product decisions, and they need <a href="/services/ecommerce-website-development/">a storefront built around them</a> rather than an app bolted onto a theme.</p>
<p>The brands that win at the premium end aren’t selling better cotton. They’re selling a feeling of rarity and belonging — and they build every pixel of the storefront to protect it.</p>
<hr>
<p><em>I’m Divyansh Sood. I built <a href="https://www.divyanshsood.com/projects/cultxberserk/">CultXberserk</a> — a hand-numbered apparel label where every garment carries an individual identity, never restocks, and earns the “limited” tag by actually meaning it. The custom storefront had to do unit-level inventory, drop state, and provenance that off-the-shelf stores simply don’t model. 9 live builds since 2022.</em></p>
<p><em>If your brand is a real drop model — or you want to build one — <a href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com">email me</a>. I’ll tell you within a day whether custom code is worth it or whether a headless Shopify setup gets you there cheaper.</em></p>
      <div class="tags">{post.tags.map((t) => <span>{t}</span>)}</div>
    </article>

    <RelatedPosts slug={slug} audience={post.audience} />

    <section class="post-foot-cta">
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        {post.audience === "indian-smb" ? (
          <>
            <h2>Working on something in India?</h2>
            <p>If a post here matches what you're building, send me the brief. WhatsApp reply in under an hour, 9 AM–9 PM IST — I'll tell you within a day if it's a fit.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://wa.me/919816091875" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Message me on WhatsApp <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#work">See the work</a>
            </div>
          </>
        ) : post.audience === "d2c-brand" ? (
          <>
            <h2>Building a brand a theme can't carry?</h2>
            <p>If your store has a model that off-the-shelf builders fight — hand-numbered drops, creator commerce, premium D2C — tell me about it. I read every brief personally.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com"
                >Email me <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#work">See the work</a>
            </div>
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            <h2>Working on something similar?</h2>
            <p>If a post here matches what you're building, send me the brief. I reply personally, usually within a couple of hours — and I'll tell you within a day if it's a fit.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com"
                >Email me <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={CALENDLY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Book a 15-min intro <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
            </div>
          </>
        )}
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Studio>
